From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Tue, 06 Oct 2026 19:41:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: drop stale link channel context in full reset

A full reset reloads the firmware, which then knows no BSS, but each
interface keeps the channel context of its default link. The hardware
restart adds the channel contexts before the links, so the channel
set sends a beacon for every AP interface before its BSS exists. On
MT7992 the 5 GHz band then sends no beacons, and later beacon updates
do not start them.

The full reset now clears that channel context, so no BSS command
reaches the firmware until mac80211 assigns the context again, the
same order as at the first start. A channel switch that completes
before that reports success without firmware commands, because an
error stops the AP; the restart adds the BSS on the new channel.

Fixes: 31f1fdb7cdbf ("wifi: mt76: mt7996: clear stale link state on full reset")

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 mt7996/mac.c  |    6 ++++++
 mt7996/main.c |    8 ++++++++
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+)

--- a/mt7996/mac.c
+++ b/mt7996/mac.c
@@ -2457,6 +2457,12 @@ mt7996_mac_reset_vif_iter(void *data, u8
 	struct mt7996_dev *dev = data;
 	int i;
 
+	/* the reloaded firmware has no BSS for this link until mac80211
+	 * assigns the channel context again, so nothing may resolve a phy
+	 * through it and send BSS commands before that
+	 */
+	mlink->ctx = NULL;
+
 	rcu_read_lock();
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(mvif->link); i++) {
 
--- a/mt7996/main.c
+++ b/mt7996/main.c
@@ -1124,6 +1124,14 @@ mt7996_post_channel_switch(struct ieee80
 	if (!link)
 		goto out;
 
+	/* after a full reset the firmware has no BSS for this link, and the
+	 * restart adds it on the new channel; an error here would stop the AP
+	 */
+	if (!mt7996_vif_link_phy(link)) {
+		mutex_unlock(&dev->mt76.mutex);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	ret = mt7996_mcu_update_bss_rfch(phy, link);
 	if (ret)
 		goto out;
